  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "tZ7D4VIPVMCl3q/INBXP6XrhDlA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:33:06.656Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "CAPE is a Mendix partner \u2014 Benelux Partner of the Year, per the workbook \u2014 and also works with eMagiz and .NET. The research's finding concerns eMagiz specifically: it gives the firm a genuine integration bench, which is the capability the pre-integration endgame depends on.\n\nThe distinction between low-code delivery and integration work is what makes this useful. Building applications on a low-code platform and connecting existing systems to one another are different skills sold to different buyers. A firm with an integration practice already sells the engagements in which a record layer between systems is a natural component rather than an unfamiliar proposal.\n\nThe qualifier the research attaches is that Mendix occupies the slot. The low-code platform is already established at CAPE's clients, so the opening is not a replacement but the integration seam beside it \u2014 and the eMagiz bench is what makes that seam somewhere the firm can actually build.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 decide thesis first**\n- CAPE (NL \u00b7 not verified)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> eMagiz gives it a genuine integration bench, which matters for the pre-integration endgame. Mendix occupies the slot.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:33:06.656622Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:33:06.656627Z"
    }
  },
